    {
      "block_id": 15,
      "type": 0,
      "text": "These registers are for bit and bit ﬁeld (1 – 32 bits), byte (8 bits), word (16 bits), long-word\n(32 bits), and quad-word (64 bits) operations. They also can be used as index registers. \n",
      "bbox": [
        63.0,
        567.0999755859375,
        543.0718994140625,
        597.592041015625
      ],
      "bbox_norm": [
        0.10294117647058823,
        0.7160353227095171,
        0.8873723846635008,
        0.7545354053227589
      ]
    },
    {
      "block_id": 16,
      "type": 0,
      "text": "1.1.2 Address Registers (A7 – A0) \n",
      "bbox": [
        63.0,
        606.35400390625,
        290.1919860839844,
        625.6319580078125
      ],
      "bbox_norm": [
        0.10294117647058823,
        0.7655984897806187,
        0.47416991190193525,
        0.7899393409189551
      ]
    },
    {
      "block_id": 17,
      "type": 0,
      "text": "These registers can be used as software stack pointers, index registers, or base address\nregisters. The base address registers can be used for word and long-word operations.\nRegister A7 is used as a hardware stack pointer during stacking for subroutine calls and\nexception handling. In the user programming model, A7 refers to the user stack pointer\n(USP). \n",
